import java.util.Arrays;

public class UnionFind {
    private int[] parent;

    public UnionFind(int size) {

        parent = new int[size];
        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
            parent[i] = i;
        }
    }

    public int find(int i) {

        if (parent[i] == i) {
            return i;
        }

        return find(parent[i]);
    }

    public void union(int i, int j) {

        int irep = find(i);

        int jrep = find(j);

        parent[irep] = jrep;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int size = 5;
        UnionFind uf = new UnionFind(size);
        uf.union(1, 2);
        uf.union(3, 4);
        boolean inSameSet = uf.find(1) == uf.find(2);
        System.out.println("Are 1 and 2 in the same set? " + inSameSet);
    }
}

Review the codebase above and ask the coding agent for your first refactoring request.

The refactoring made three targeted changes:

1. **Added `rank[]` array** — initialized to all zeros alongside `parent[]`.

2. **Path compression in `find`** — changed from following the chain on every call to flattening it in place. The recursive call now writes the root directly back into `parent[i]`, so subsequent finds on any node along that path go straight to the root in O(1).

3. **Union by rank** — instead of always attaching `irep` under `jrep`, the lower-rank tree is attached under the higher-rank one. When ranks are equal, one becomes the root and its rank increments. Added an early return when both nodes are already in the same set.

Together these give amortized O(α(n)) per operation (inverse Ackermann), compared to the original O(n) worst-case per `find`.

